studio: the idea, in five lines

The goal, first: one outline, two renderers
  sys has grown pieces that each read and draw notes their own way: three
  programs read a note's text, and two make a todo page. The first goal
  is fewer. One outline runs from the root through folders and files to a
  single item of a note (README:/The outline does not stop at a file/). One
  reader builds it, medium/page/list. Two views draw it, the terminal's,
  which gio's window draws too, and the page in the browser, with one
  cursor: on the terminal fm walks the folders and ed beside it edits the
  file, and the page folds both.
  fm and ed run in either view: what they do does not change with where it
  is shown. A primitive is added once, to the reader, and both views draw
  it. A piece that serves none of this goes.

Built on sys, not beside it
  The studio is ed, fm and the rest of sys; studio, the program, only lays
  them out (studio/frame). ed edits a thing, fm moves between things, tty
  is the terminal both stand on, and tangle stitches a page from its parts.
  A lens shows what is derived from a thing, never what is typed, which is
  ed's (design/todo:/typed at rest/).
  A need goes into one of those first, as a few lines, and becomes a new
  piece only when it fits none of them. The terminal pane runs fm and ed as
  they are (todo/studio/terminal:/fm, ed, c and tmux/); the thing every
  lens is on is fm's cur, which c names (README:/the word you are on/).

  Target: the shelf reads, and page/py is a lens on a page; studio lays
  fm, ed and a shell out in tmux, and studio show is the lenses there. No
  page holds them all yet.

  1  a thing is a file or a folder
  2  a lens shows a thing          source, outline, graph, run, diff
  3  an id joins the lenses
  4  the shelf reads               list, show, flip, follow
  5  the terminal acts, and writes a file a lens shows

The todo is where it starts (`design/todo`): the things you mean to do,
as a tree, in the order you do them. All five lines hold there already:
its nodes are files and folders, its page and its walk are lenses, an id
is the path down, the page only reads, and ed types the state. What the
studio can take from it, still open: `todo/studio/entry`.

The case, carried through: medium/page/py, node
  medium/page/ is the studio's own source: the renderers, their check, a
  README. `studio show` draws the lenses on it in the terminal, and the
  page will at step 8 (`todo/studio/outline:/a page view of fm and ed/`).

  thing     page/py : node                             the id
  source    node as written, lines 311..328            nothing makes it
  outline   def node(d, lines, marks)  18, 1 in 6 out  page/py
  graph     held -> node -> esc heading held hover skyline source
            held calls node again: a tree, so a cycle  ast, one file
  run       45 checks, 0 failed                        node run.mjs
  diff      node before the refactor, +12 -11          difflib

  folder    the same thing one level up: lens = rows | thumbs | changes

A new idea fits if it is a lens on a thing, or a verb the terminal runs.
If it is neither, it does not belong.

Not built: follow (callers and callees at the edge), the graph, the run lens.
After the goal: follow. It needs no new picture; it is the join of diff and
graph. Then: run, starting with tests, which already write a file.

Open
